\description{
Runs a Gibbs sampler for a causal model in which one or more latent person
traits \code{theta}, each measured by its own two-parameter logistic (2PL)
item-response model, confound a binary treatment \code{z} and a continuous outcome
\code{y}. Within each scan:
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item Each trait gets one per-person random-walk Metropolis move (the \code{theta_j}
are conditionally independent given the trees and item parameters, so each
person is accepted or rejected on its own). The traits are visited in a
systematic scan --- trait \code{k} is proposed and installed for every person
before trait \code{k + 1} is touched. The accepted values are installed into both
BART models by a joint per-observation sweep
(\code{\link[dbarts:updatePredictorPerObservationJointly]{dbarts::updatePredictorPerObservationJointly()}}): a person is kept only
if its \code{theta} leaves every leaf non-empty in every tree of both models.
\item The two BART surfaces --- \code{y ~ f(z, theta)} (response) and
\code{z ~ f(theta)} (probit assignment) --- each take one tree-update step.
\item The IRT item parameters \verb{(alpha, beta)} of each bank are drawn conditional
on that bank's trait by the WALNUTS sampler (\code{\link[=irt_item_sampler]{irt_item_sampler()}}):
adapting during burn-in, then frozen for the sampling phase. Conditional on
the traits the banks are independent, so this is one sampler per bank, not
one larger one.
\item With covariates, the latent-regression coefficients \code{gamma} are drawn from
their exact (conjugate normal) full conditional.
}

The treatment-effect estimand \code{E[f(1, theta) - f(0, theta)]} is accumulated
from the response surface each sampling scan.
}
\section{Assumptions}{


The returned \code{ate} is a causal effect only under the following. They are
strong, and the package cannot check any of them.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Items are measured before treatment.} If the responses postdate \code{z},
\code{theta} is a mediator or a collider rather than a confounder, and
conditioning on it can move the estimate in either direction. This is the
assumption most easily violated in practice and the one with the worst
consequences.
\item \strong{\verb{(theta, x)} is the whole confounder.} \code{Y(z)} must be independent of \code{Z}
given the latent trait(s) and whatever is passed as \code{x}. Confounding routed
through neither is unadjusted, and with \code{x = NULL} that means the trait
alone is being asked to carry all of it.
\item \strong{Overlap:} \verb{0 < P(Z = 1 | theta) < 1} across the support of \code{theta}.
\item \strong{Exclusion:} the item responses are pure measures of \code{theta}, with no
direct path to \code{y} or \code{z}. Differential item functioning by treatment group
violates this.
\item \strong{The measurement model is correct} --- 2PL, local independence, one trait
per bank. This is what does the identifying work, so misspecification is
not benign: adjusting for a mismeasured confounder leaves residual
confounding whose direction is not guaranteed to be conservative (Greenland
1980; Ogburn and VanderWeele 2012). Fewer or noisier items attenuate the
estimate toward zero, the usual measurement-error signature.
\item \strong{SUTVA}, as for any such estimand.
}

Because the estimand is a contrast of the response surface at \code{z = 1} and
\code{z = 0}, it is invariant to the location and scale conventions of the latent
metric --- unlike "the effect of a one-SD increase in \code{theta}", which is not.

A useful comparator, requiring weaker assumptions than the 2PL, is to put the
sum score (or the raw responses) directly into the outcome surface. If the
two disagree, the measurement model is doing the work and deserves scrutiny.
}

